<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fernand Braudel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1902">1902</a>–<a href="/years/1985">1985</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fernand Braudel (1902–1985) was a French historian and leading figure of the Annales school. His The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the Mediterranean World in the Age of Philip II (1949) studied history at the levels of geography, slow economic and social structures, and events, and his later Civilisation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">Capitalism</a> traced the world economy from the fifteenth to the eighteenth century, giving prominence to the Genoese bankers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fernand-braudel.jpg" alt="Fernand Braudel" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jerry Bauer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photo_of_Fernand_Braudel_on_The_Identity_of_France_dustjacket.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fernand Braudel was born on 24 August 1902 in Luméville-en-Ornois, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, and joined his father, a mathematics teacher, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> at the age of seven. He studied at the Sorbonne and gained his agrégation in history in 1923, then taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> until 1932. There he became fascinated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and began a doctoral thesis on the foreign policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>, working in archives at Simancas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>. In 1935 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> to help build the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a>. On the voyage home in 1937 he travelled with Lucien Febvre, co-founder of the journal Annales, and came under the influence of the Annales School.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Called up in 1939, Braudel was taken prisoner on 29 June 1940 and held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, where he became rector of the camp university, and from June 1942 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>. He drafted his work on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> in captivity, without his own books and notes, sending copy books to Febvre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. After 1945 he led the second generation of Annales historians, defended his thesis in 1947 and was elected to the Collège de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1949. He headed the Sixième section of the École pratique des hautes études from 1956 and edited the Annales from 1957. He directed the Fondation Maison des Sciences de l'Homme from 1970 until his death and was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academie-francaise">Académie française</a> in 1984.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the Mediterranean World in the Age of Philip II (1949) was his most influential book. For Braudel there is no single Mediterranean but many seas, bound to the plains, mountains and desert around them. He divided time into three levels: the slow time of geography, the social and economic history of two or three centuries, and the history of events, which he called the time of surfaces.</p><p>Civilization and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">Capitalism</a>, 15th–18th Century (1967–1979) follows how ordinary people made economies work, and traces centres of capitalism in turn: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. He argued that capitalists were typically monopolists, not entrepreneurs in competitive markets. His last book, The Identity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, was unfinished at his death on 27 November 1985. He stressed constraints on human action and the lives of slaves, serfs, peasants and the urban poor, and is regarded as a precursor of world-systems theory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fernand Braudel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The historian <b>Fernand Braudel</b> spoke of an age of the Genoese from 1557 to 1627, when Genoese bankers handled the silver of the Spanish Empire and the exchange fairs at Piacenza became the clearing house of European finance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Fernand Braudel</b> argued that capitalism, in which a few use capital to control markets, is to some degree opposed to free market exchange.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Luméville-en-Ornois, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Cluses, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.47,5.43&amp;year=1955" aria-label="Luméville-en-Ornois on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4676 469 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Fernand Braudel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5126 739h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5126 739h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.47,5.43&amp;year=1955">What was here in 1955? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
